  "query_preview": "..Current radar shows scattered thunderstorms east\nof Tucson with a few isolated storms west and north of Tucson\nover Pinal County and central Pima County. The latest HRRR run\nseems to be handling the convection east of Tucson well, but\ndoes not show any of the convection west and north of Tucson.\nThese storms are likely being aided by a weak shortwave moving\nthrough central Arizona. Storms east of Tucson will likely keep\nmoving north and produce moderate to heavy rainfall. At this \nmoment it is uncertain if Tucson will see any storms, there are \na couple outflows heading towards the metro from the east as \nwell as the north. This could kick off storms over the metro, \nbut confidence is low. This activity will likely continue \nthrough the early morning, then a brief break or reduction in \ncoverage by mid to late morning. CAMs are showing another round \nof scattered storms forming this afternoon and evening, but \nactivity from this morning could delay initiation and reduce \ncoverage or change where storms form. Still some uncertainty in \nthe forecast, but the threat is there for scattered convection. \nHeavy rainfall leading to flash flooding looks to be the main \nthreat with strong to severe wind gusts a secondary threat. For \nthis afternoon and evening hi-res guidance is favoring areas \nwest of Tucson over the Tohono O'Odham Nation. Because of this \nthe Flash Flood Watch has been extended west of Tucson through \n10 PM today. \n\nAfter today moisture values decrease some thanks to southwest\nflow aloft ahead of trough over the west coast. The ridge will \nbe centered well to the east. The upper air pattern will remain \nlike this through the work week. Expect a downturn in monsoon \nthunderstorm activity and coverage Tuesday and Wednesday with \nisolated storms mainly south and east of Tucson. High \ntemperatures will be 2-6 degrees cooler than normal today into \nWednesday. \n\nDown in the tropics the NHC is monitoring a tropical wave with \ndisorganized showers and thunderstorms well south of SW Mexico.\nThis will likely become the next named eastern Pacific tropical\nstorm in a couple days. Models bring this tropical system north\nalong the west coast of Baja late in the work week into the \nweekend. Still fairly large differences in how close to the \ncoast the center will be. However there is growing confidence \nthat it will send tropical moisture up to southeast Arizona. \nPWATs begin to increase as early as Thursday and could reach as \nhigh as 2\" over far western Pima County by this weekend. This \nfar out still hard to pin down exact impacts from this potential\nincrease in moisture, but do expect an increase in thunderstorm\ncoverage and chances as early as Thursday, continuing into the \nweekend.",
